<spell-notebook "extra usage">
	<header level="1">
		Extra
		<header>Extra content is set apart from the main content, such as a card's footer.</>
	</>
	<extra-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#owners">Owners</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-extra>` is extra content, set apart from the main content: a card's footer, an item's extra line, a feed
			event's extra text or images.

			- In a card it is a bottom band, divided from the content above, muted until hovered.
			- In a feed, `text` makes it a block of quoted text; without it, it holds images or other markup.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Owners">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-extra>` finds its nearest owner and sets `:state(in-<owner>)` on itself; `UIParts.css` styles it by that
			state. Its owners, and what each makes of it:

			- [Card](/components/ui/ui-card.html) (`:state(in-card)`): the bottom band, divided from the content and muted
			- [Item](/components/ui/ui-items.html) (`:state(in-item)`): a muted line under the description
			- [Feed](/components/ui/ui-feed.html) (`:state(in-feed)`): extra text (`text`) or images under the summary

			Anywhere else it is an unstyled block. More on how parts find their owners: [Content parts](/components/ui/ui-header.html).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>- A neutral block with no role. Its muted colour is a look only: put nothing in it that a reader must not miss.</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
